<p>The popular image of <strong>how Japanese Wagyu cattle are raised</strong> is filled with memorable stories: cows drinking beer, daily massages, classical music and a life resembling a spa. Those stories are easy to repeat but poor explanations of real production. Wagyu does not develop marbling because of one universal ritual. The result comes from genetics, selection, feeding, time, management and carefully organised production and traceability systems.</p>
<p>There is no single farm that represents all of Japan either. Feed ingredients, housing, schedules and management differ by producer and region. The useful approach is to understand <strong>the main stages and logic of production</strong> without turning individual farm practices into rules for every animal.</p>
<h2>It begins before birth: genetics and selection</h2>
<p>A Wagyu animal's potential is influenced by genetics. Japan recognises four Wagyu breeds: Japanese Black, Japanese Brown, Japanese Shorthorn and Japanese Polled, together with recognised crosses within the relevant definition. Japanese Black, or Kuroge Washu, is by far the breed most associated with extreme marbling.</p>
<p>Breeders select breeding animals using pedigree and performance information. Selection cannot guarantee that every descendant will grade A5 because the final result depends on many factors, but generations of genetic improvement provide the foundation of the system.</p>
<p>See <a href="/en/four-japanese-wagyu-breeds/">the four Japanese Wagyu breeds</a> and <a href="/en/kuroge-washu-japanese-black-wagyu/">Kuroge Washu</a>.</p>
<h2>The calf-rearing stage</h2>
<p>During the first months, the priority is healthy growth and development. Calves require colostrum, milk or appropriate early feeding systems, followed by a gradual transition to forage and concentrates according to the farm programme.</p>
<p>Digestive health, bone development and adaptation to later feeding matter. It would make little sense to chase maximum marbling from birth at the expense of overall development. Premium beef production is sequential.</p>
<h2>Different farms can specialise in different stages</h2>
<p>The Japanese Wagyu chain can separate calf production from finishing. Some farmers specialise in breeding cows and young calves; others purchase young feeder cattle and carry out the long finishing period. Livestock markets and relationships between specialised producers form part of this structure.</p>
<p>One farm story therefore does not necessarily describe an animal's whole life. Traceability helps retain a connection to the animal's history across movements and production stages.</p>
<h2>The growing stage prepares cattle for finishing</h2>
<p>Documented Japanese producers describe young cattle entering finishing farms at roughly eight to ten months in some systems. Early in that period, attention may be given to forage and digestive development before concentrated feed is increased gradually.</p>
<p>That age should not be turned into a universal rule. Different farms use different calendars. The important principle is progression: cattle need to adapt, grow and remain healthy enough to sustain a long finishing period.</p>
<h2>What do cattle actually eat during finishing?</h2>
<p>Diets can include forage and mixed concentrate feeds containing ingredients such as corn, barley, soybean meal and other components. The exact formula varies by producer, availability, stage and nutritional goal.</p>
<p>Rations are designed to supply energy, protein, fibre, minerals and other nutrients. A single “secret Wagyu menu” is an oversimplification. Even farms in the same prefecture may use different feeds.</p>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/en/what-do-wagyu-cattle-eat-beer-massage-myths/">what Wagyu cattle eat</a> explores the subject in detail.</p>
<h2>So they do not drink beer?</h2>
<p>Beer is not a general requirement for producing Wagyu or Kobe Beef. It may have existed as an occasional practice on particular farms, but treating it as the cause of marbling is incorrect. The same applies to massage and music.</p>
<p>Those myths distract from factors that genuinely matter: genetics, total nutrition over many months, age, management and selection. A particular drink does not turn an animal into A5.</p>
<h2>Finishing is long and gradual</h2>
<p>Japanese producers have described systems where cattle are finished to around 29 or 30 months of age before shipment. Other programmes vary. The useful lesson is not one rigid number but the fact that high-quality Wagyu develops over an extended period.</p>
<p>That time allows body growth and gradual fat deposition, including intramuscular fat. It also increases feed, housing and management costs, contributing to the eventual value of the beef.</p>
<h2>How marbling develops</h2>
<p>Marbling is fat deposited within muscle. Its development depends on genetic predisposition and the interaction of nutrition, growth, age and metabolism. It does not appear because somebody massages the animal's surface or artificially prevents movement to “make fat.”</p>
<p>A large amount of visible fat is not the only quality requirement either. Japanese grading evaluates other characteristics of meat and fat. See <a href="/en/why-wagyu-has-so-much-marbling/">why Wagyu develops so much marbling</a>.</p>
<h2>Daily management has a basic objective: healthy animals</h2>
<p>In any serious cattle system, monitoring appetite, behaviour, movement, body condition and signs of illness is fundamental. A long finishing period requires problems to be detected early and feeding or care adjusted when necessary.</p>
<p>Individual attention can be especially important where each animal represents months of work and significant potential value. That does not mean treating cattle as pets; it means managing production animals whose health affects welfare and economic outcome.</p>
<h2>Stress is not a recipe for marbling</h2>
<p>It is sometimes claimed that Wagyu cattle “must never be stressed or they stop marbling.” The relationship between stress and meat quality is more complex. Poor handling, particularly around transport and slaughter, can affect glycogen reserves, pH and final beef quality in cattle.</p>
<p>That should not be turned into the simplistic idea that a particular type of music or massage produces higher BMS. Good handling and welfare matter in their own right and in relation to health and quality, not as a magical marbling technique.</p>
<h2>Traceability from the animal to the beef</h2>
<p>Japan uses individual cattle identification. The identification number connects records to the animal throughout the chain. For consumers, this traceability helps verify that beef genuinely comes from the Japanese system being claimed.</p>
<p>Certain regional brands add further rules and controls. Kobe is the best-known international example, but not all Japanese Wagyu is Kobe.</p>
<p>See <a href="/en/japanese-wagyu-certificate-traceability/">Japanese Wagyu certification and traceability</a>.</p>
<h2>After rearing comes carcass grading</h2>
<p>A farmer's work does not automatically translate into an A5 label. After slaughter and carcass preparation, the relevant grading process is applied. Japan combines yield grades A, B or C with quality grades 1 through 5.</p>
<p>Quality includes marbling, colour and brightness, firmness and texture, and fat characteristics. Two cattle raised under similar programmes can therefore receive different final grades.</p>
<h2>There is no single “standard Wagyu farm”</h2>
<p>Japan has different production regions, climates, farm sizes and traditions. Some farmers raise relatively few cattle while others manage larger groups. Feed can be adapted to local resources and regional brands can establish their own additional requirements.</p>
<p>It is more accurate to discuss principles and systems than one identical routine. When a commercial description claims that “all Wagyu cattle” receive an extravagant treatment, ask for the source.</p>
<h2>What this means when buying the beef</h2>
<p>Rearing matters because it explains why Wagyu cannot be evaluated from a photograph alone. Genetics, origin and traceability provide context. Grade and BMS describe the carcass result, while cut and format determine the cooking experience.</p>
<p>On a product page, origin, breed when known, grade, cut and traceability are more useful than a generic story about beer and massages.</p>
<h2>The conclusion</h2>
<p><strong>Raising Japanese Wagyu is a long process of selection, growth, feeding, management and control, not a collection of exotic rituals.</strong> Different farms and production stages may be involved, and specific methods vary among producers.</p>
<p>Exceptional marbling develops when genetics and production interact over many months. Understanding the process makes the beef easier to value and reveals a Wagyu paradox: the farming reality is less extravagant than the myth, but considerably more interesting.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Are all Wagyu cattle raised in the same way?</h3>
<p>No. Feed, housing, duration and management vary by farm and region, although common identification, production and grading systems provide structure.</p>
<h3>Do Wagyu cattle drink beer?</h3>
<p>It is not a general requirement. Isolated practices may exist, but beer does not define Wagyu and is not the single cause of marbling.</p>
<h3>How long are Wagyu cattle raised?</h3>
<p>Production can be prolonged, and some Japanese finishing systems describe cattle leaving at around 29–30 months, but that should not be treated as a universal age for every Wagyu animal.</p>
<h3>Does marbling depend only on feed?</h3>
<p>No. Genetics, selection, age, nutrition, growth and metabolism interact in the development of intramuscular fat.</p>
